User-triggerable WARN: `ctlv_input_attr()` on a cursor whose attr is `NULL` warns. A `NULL`-attr nest cursor is exactly what `ctlv_input_attr()`/`CTLV_IN_NEST` produce when an optional nest is absent from the request (`indexed->count` == 0). A handler that then calls any `_get()`/`_present()` on that nested cursor, which is the natural "optional nest absent => defaults" pattern, hits this WARN with a request chosen by userspace (`panic_on_warn` = DoS, and syzkaller noise otherwise). An absent nest should simply yield an absent child, silently.

Also aliasing: *cursor is overwritten with the empty state before nest is read, so `ctlv_input_attr(&c, &c, ref)` (descend in place) reads a zeroed nest and WARNs. Build the result in a local and assign at the end, or document that cursor != nest is required.

